How Material Physics Determines Commercial Demand
Radio-frequency electronics, optical connectivity and high-voltage power conversion create distinct requirements. III–V materials are used in specialized photonic and microwave components, while SiC provides attractive electrical and thermal properties for demanding power stages. Higher device requirements can increase demand for specialized substrates, but the customer’s yield remains critical.

Growing a high-quality crystal, slicing it and polishing it to a qualified surface creates substantial technical difficulty. Defect density, wafer bow, dislocation behavior and resistivity specification can drive rejection before a device process begins. Material suppliers win repeat orders by demonstrating consistent usable wafer area and supplying technical documentation.

